      "question": "What is the most likely diagnosis based on the history, physical findings, and microscopy?",
      "my_answer": "Classic scabies (Sarcoptes scabiei infestation).",
      "rationale": "Intense nocturnal pruritus, involvement of typical sites (interdigital web spaces, wrists, axillae, periumbilical area), visible linear burrows, and demonstration of mites, eggs, and fecal pellets on mineral oil preparation are pathognomonic for scabies."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q2",
      "question": "How urgent is treatment for this condition?",
      "my_answer": "Requires non‑emergent care; treatment should be started promptly (within days) to relieve symptoms and prevent spread, but it is not a medical emergency.",
      "rationale": "Scabies is not life‑threatening, but delayed therapy leads to worsening itching, sleep disruption, secondary bacterial infection, and household transmission."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q3",
      "question": "Which clinical specialty should initially manage this patient?",
      "my_answer": "Pediatrics (primary‑care pediatrician).",
      "rationale": "Scabies is a common dermatologic condition that can be diagnosed and treated by a pediatrician; dermatology referral is reserved for treatment failure, atypical presentations, or crusted scabies."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q4",
      "question": "What are the key conditions on the differential diagnosis for pruritic papules with burrows in a child?",
      "my_answer": "Atopic dermatitis, allergic contact dermatitis, papular urticaria, pediculosis (lice), impetigo secondary to scratching, fungal intertrigo, insect bites, and early crusted scabies.",
      "rationale": "These entities can produce pruritic papules or linear lesions, but they lack the classic burrows and microscopic confirmation of mites seen here."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q5",
      "question": "What red‑flag findings would necessitate urgent referral or escalation of care?",
      "my_answer": "Signs of secondary bacterial infection (e.g., crusted lesions, erythema, warmth, purulent drainage), crusted (Norwegian) scabies, immunocompromised state, extensive involvement covering >50% of body surface, fever, or failure to improve after two courses of therapy.",
      "rationale": "These features indicate possible complications, atypical disease severity, or treatment failure that require dermatology or infectious‑disease input and possibly systemic therapy."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q6",
      "question": "What diagnostic test(s) are required to confirm the diagnosis?",
      "my_answer": "Skin‑scraping with mineral oil preparation examined under microscopy (demonstration of mites, eggs, or feces) – already performed and positive. Dermoscopy can be an adjunct but is not required.",
      "rationale": "Microscopic identification of the mite is the gold‑standard confirmatory test; the case already provides this evidence."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q7",
      "question": "What is the first‑line pharmacologic treatment for this patient?",
      "my_answer": "Topical permethrin 5% cream applied to the entire body from neck down (including scalp in children <2 y, but not needed here), left on for 8‑14 hours, then washed off; repeat a second application 7‑10 days later.",
      "rationale": "Permethrin 5% is the recommended first‑line agent for classic scabies in children ≥2 years; it is safe, highly effective, and requires only two applications."
    },
    {
      "id": "Q8",
      "question": "What non‑pharmacologic measures and follow‑up plan should be instituted?",
      "my_answer": "Treat all household contacts simultaneously, wash all clothing/bedding in hot water and dry on high heat or seal in plastic bags for 72 h, apply soothing measures (e.g., oral antihistamines, cool compresses), advise avoidance of scratching to reduce secondary infection, and schedule a follow‑up visit in 2‑3 weeks to assess symptom resolution and check for complications.",
      "rationale": "Scabies is highly contagious; simultaneous treatment and environmental decontamination prevent reinfestation. Follow‑up ensures therapeutic success and identifies any red‑flag complications."
    }
  ],
